<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>A 50-year-old professional boxing referee and boxing instructor with newly found stage 2 hypertension, left ventricular hypertrophy by voltage on the ECG, and two months of breathlessness and chest tightness late in long nights of refereeing in hot arenas. Please evaluate the symptoms and the hypertrophy, advise on his coronary and sudden cardiac death risk, and say whether and how he can keep refereeing. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Soon, within two weeks; he is refereeing only short undercards until seen. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Are his exertional breathlessness and chest tightness ischemic, and what test do you advise?</item><item>Is the left ventricular hypertrophy from hypertension, or could it be hypertrophic cardiomyopathy or an athlete&apos;s heart?</item><item>What is his risk of sudden cardiac death, and does he need ambulatory monitoring or other testing?</item><item>Would a coronary calcium score help decide on a statin and aspirin, and what do you recommend?</item><item>Can he keep refereeing, which means hours of moving about the ring in hot arenas, and with what precautions?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Leonard, 50, a professional boxing referee and boxing instructor, is seen at the request of Dr. Okafor-Rosenthal two weeks after stage 2 hypertension was found at his license examination. For about two months he has had breathlessness and chest tightness late in long nights of refereeing in hot arenas, easing with a few minutes&apos; rest; none at rest, no fainting or palpitations. Since starting losartan-hydrochlorothiazide two weeks ago he has refereed only undercards and has had no symptoms. He never smoked. His family history is not known. He asked whether he will lose his license. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Benjamin Leiner was born on April 7, 1896, in New York City, to Gershon Leiner, a tailor, and Minnie Leiner, Orthodox Jewish immigrants from Russia, and grew up on the Lower East Side of Manhattan. [D]</item><item>He began fighting professionally at about fifteen and took the name Benny Leonard to keep his boxing secret from his parents, because boxing was not thought a respectable living in their community. [D]</item><item>He won the world lightweight championship from Freddie Welsh on May 28, 1917, and held it until he retired as undefeated champion in January 1925, at his mother&apos;s wish. [D]</item><item>The son of Orthodox Jews, he never fought on a Jewish holiday and became a symbol of the acceptance of Jews in American life at a time of open antisemitism; the newspaper editor Arthur Brisbane wrote that he had done more to conquer anti-Semitism than a thousand textbooks. He later supported the Maccabiah Games. [D]</item><item>Retired, he had become wealthy; in his own words he was practically a millionaire, without a scratch. [D]</item><item>He lost most of his savings in the 1929 stock market crash and in October 1931, at 35, came back as a welterweight; he won about nineteen fights in a row before Jimmy McLarnin knocked him out in the sixth round in October 1932, and he retired for good. [D]</item><item>During the Second World War he served in the U.S. Maritime Service of the Merchant Marine, rising to lieutenant commander. [D]</item><item>He returned to boxing as a referee of professional bouts in New York and also taught boxing at City College of New York. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

