<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Exertional breathlessness and chest tightness (R06.09). Not ischemic. An exercise stress echocardiogram was chosen because it tests for ischemia, measures his working capacity and shows the blood pressure response to effort; it showed no inducible ischemia at about 10.5 METs. The symptoms are best explained by a steep rise in blood pressure on exertion (to 226/98 mmHg) in a thickened, stiff ventricle, made worse by heat; they should settle as blood pressure is controlled. [S]</item><item>Type of hypertrophy (I11.9). Hypertensive heart disease. The hypertrophy is concentric and modest (septum 13 mm, septal-to-posterior ratio 1.1) with no systolic anterior motion, no outflow gradient, a normal-sized cavity and diastolic dysfunction, in a man with stage 2 hypertension; this fits hypertension and not hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and an athlete&apos;s heart is unlikely at 50 years and years after competitive training. If the hypertrophy does not regress after six to twelve months of good control, or a family history emerges, cardiac MRI would be the next test. [S]</item><item>Sudden cardiac death risk (I11.9). Left ventricular hypertrophy raises the risk of sudden cardiac death, and controlling blood pressure lowers it as the hypertrophy regresses. He has no fainting, palpitations or arrhythmia on exercise, a normal ejection fraction and a normal QTc, so ambulatory ECG monitoring and an implantable defibrillator are not indicated; monitoring would be needed for palpitations or fainting. Potassium should be kept in the normal range on the diuretic. He should know the warning symptoms, and the venues where he works should have a defibrillator at ringside and staff trained in CPR. [S]</item><item>Coronary calcium, statin and aspirin (I25.10). A coronary calcium score of 186, above 100 and about the 80th percentile, shows coronary atherosclerosis and favors a statin: atorvastatin 40 mg, aiming for LDL below 70 mg/dL and a fall of at least 50%, with a lipid panel at eight to twelve weeks. Low-dose aspirin may be considered with a score over 100, but not while blood pressure is uncontrolled because of the bleeding risk; it should be revisited as a shared decision once blood pressure is at goal. Routine repeat stress testing is not recommended without new symptoms. [S]</item><item>Refereeing (I11.9). He can keep refereeing. Until home blood pressure is below 140/90 mmHg, short undercards only; then full cards. Drink water between bouts, especially in hot arenas; stop and hand over a bout for chest discomfort, faintness or palpitations; hold the blood pressure tablet on sick days. A thiazide can deplete salt and water in a man who sweats heavily at work; if that happens, losartan with amlodipine is a good alternative. The target is below 130/80 mmHg, and the dose of losartan-hydrochlorothiazide can be raised at the next review if he is not there. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Okafor-Rosenthal&apos;s intake note, ECG and laboratory tests. Home readings over the past week averaged 146/92 mmHg. Basic metabolic panel today: sodium 139, potassium 4.0 mmol/L, creatinine 1.04 mg/dL. Transthoracic echocardiogram today: concentric left ventricular hypertrophy (septum 13 mm, posterior wall 12 mm, mass index 118 g/m2), ejection fraction 62%, grade 1 diastolic dysfunction, left atrial volume index 36 mL/m2, no systolic anterior motion or outflow obstruction at rest or with Valsalva, normal valves and aortic root. Exercise stress echocardiogram today (Bruce): 9 minutes 30 seconds, about 10.5 METs, peak heart rate 156 per minute (92% of predicted), blood pressure 150/92 rising to 226/98 mmHg, mild breathlessness at peak without chest discomfort, no ST change, no arrhythmia, normal wall augmentation. Coronary calcium CT today: Agatston 186 (left anterior descending 118, right coronary 52, circumflex 16), about the 80th percentile for his age and sex. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

