    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Continue losartan 50 mg with hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg; raise the dose if home readings stay above 130/80 mmHg; basic metabolic panel after each change. [S]</item><item>If the thiazide causes volume depletion or low potassium in the heat, change to losartan with amlodipine. [S]</item><item>Atorvastatin 40 mg each evening; lipid panel at eight to twelve weeks; LDL goal below 70 mg/dL. [S]</item><item>No aspirin until blood pressure is controlled; then revisit it as a shared decision. [S]</item><item>No ambulatory ECG monitoring or defibrillator now; monitoring for any palpitations or fainting; no routine repeat stress testing. [S]</item><item>Short undercards until home blood pressure is below 140/90 mmHg, then full cards, with water between bouts and the warning symptoms explained; a defibrillator and CPR-trained staff at ringside. [S]</item><item>Repeat echocardiogram six to twelve months after today to judge regression; cardiac MRI if the hypertrophy does not regress. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Okafor-Rosenthal, who saw him the same day. Mr. Leonard was told that his heart arteries are not narrowed enough to cause his symptoms, that the thickened heart muscle comes from high blood pressure and should improve with treatment, and that he can keep refereeing with the plan above. He was relieved, and asked that the medicines be generics he can afford; all three suggested are. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Cardiology review with the repeat echocardiogram in six to twelve months, or sooner for palpitations, fainting or new chest symptoms; Dr. Okafor-Rosenthal continues his blood pressure care. [S]</item></list></text>

